  "thumbnailAlt": "Public Records Request form beside a photo of a desk lamp lighting a newspaper on a desk",
  "content": {
    "intro": "A public records request form is a form that collects a requester's contact details, a clear description of the records they want, the date range, the department that holds them and the preferred delivery format so a government agency can search for and release records under open records law. This template prompts requesters to be specific, which helps clerks find the right records and avoid long back-and-forth.",
    "audience": "City and county clerks, records officers, school districts, police departments, public universities and other agencies that handle FOIA or open records requests.",
    "useCases": [
      "The online records request page on a city or county website",
      "Police and fire departments handling report requests",
      "School districts receiving requests for board records and emails",
      "Small towns replacing paper request forms at the clerk's counter"
    ],
    "tips": [
      "Use the response table and edit history to track each request's status and keep a dated log of when it was received and closed.",
      "Send requesters an automatic email copy of their submission so they have a dated receipt of the request.",
      "Embed the form on your records page next to your fee schedule and response timelines so requesters know what to expect."
    ],
    "faq": [
      {
        "question": "What should a public records request form include?",
        "answer": "The requester's name and contact details, a specific description of the records wanted, a date range, the department or office likely to hold them, the preferred format (electronic copies, paper copies or in-person inspection), any fee limit, and whether a fee waiver is requested and why."
      },
      {
        "question": "Do I have to say why I want public records?",
        "answer": "In the US, most federal and state open records laws do not require a reason for a request, though a reason may help with a fee waiver. Rules differ between countries and states, so check the law that applies to the agency."
      },
      {
        "question": "How long does an agency have to respond to a records request?",
        "answer": "It depends on the law. The federal FOIA generally allows 20 working days, while state laws range from a few days to a 'reasonable time'. Agencies can often extend the deadline for large requests, so check your local rules."
      },
      {
        "question": "How do I make a records request more likely to succeed?",
        "answer": "Be specific: name the type of document, the people or offices involved, keywords and a narrow date range. Broad requests like 'all emails' take longer, may cost more and are more likely to be narrowed or refused."
      }
    ]
  },
